1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,740
This is doing and feeling. Episode 2, Feeling. Last time was intelligence. Now we get to the part the thread was really asking about. Where do we start?

2
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:24,340
With the most recommended item in the whole thread. One reply described it as 16 pages, a 30-minute read, and a must. It's Thomas Nagel's essay from 1974, What Is It Like to Be a Bat?

3
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:25,740
Why a bat?

4
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:31,320
because bats perceive the world through echolocation. They shriek and build a picture

5
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:36,580
of their surroundings from the echoes. Imagine we knew everything about the physics of sonar

6
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:41,840
and everything about the bat's brain. Nagel's point is that something would still be missing,

7
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:47,520
what it is like for the bat to experience the world that way. He proposed that an organism

8
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:52,180
is conscious if there is something it is like to be that organism. That phrase,

9
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:57,320
something it is like, became the standard definition. And his worry was that science,

10
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:02,400
by design, describes everything from the outside, from no particular point of view.

11
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:06,620
Experience is the one thing that only exists from the inside.

12
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:09,700
That sounds more like a puzzle than a theory.

13
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:17,480
It's the puzzle every theory has to answer. In 1995, the philosopher David Chalmers sharpened

14
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:22,180
into what he called the hard problem of consciousness. He contrasted it with the easy

15
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:28,820
problems, which are only easy by comparison. Those are about explaining functions, how the brain tells

16
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:35,460
stimuli apart, combines information, directs attention, and produces reports. Those are hard

17
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:40,540
science, but we know what an answer would look like. It would be a mechanism. The hard problem

18
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:46,100
is different. Why is any of that processing accompanied by experience at all, instead of

19
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:50,140
And this is where the zombies come in.

20
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:51,080
Right.

21
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:55,040
Chalmers asks you to imagine a being that's physically identical to you,

22
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:59,980
atom for atom, and behaves exactly like you, but has no experience whatsoever.

23
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:02,000
A philosophical zombie.

24
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:06,060
If such a being is even coherently conceivable, he argues,

25
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:10,140
then consciousness is not logically guaranteed by the physical facts,

26
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:12,660
and a purely physical story is incomplete.

27
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:15,960
His book The Conscious Mind lays all of this out.

28
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:22,940
from Frank Jackson. Mary is a brilliant color scientist who knows every physical fact about

29
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:27,900
color vision, but she has lived her whole life in a blackened white room. When she walks out

30
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:32,480
and sees a red tomato for the first time, does she learn something new? If she does,

31
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:37,960
then the physical facts were not all the facts. Jackson himself later changed his mind and became

32
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:43,320
a physicalist, but the argument outlived his support for it. I can already hear the engineers

33
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:49,040
in that thread pushing back. And they should, because the strongest replies to Chalmers are

34
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:54,100
some of the best things on your list. They're also exactly what the Grok summary you were given left

35
00:02:54,100 --> 00:03:00,020
out. So let's draw a map. I'll go roughly from consciousness is nothing over and above information

36
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:05,760
processing, all the way to consciousness is the most fundamental thing there is. First stop,

37
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:12,000
functionalism. Mental states are defined by what they do, by their causal roles, not by what they're

38
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:19,260
is whatever state is caused by damage and in turn causes avoidance, distress, and saying ouch.

39
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:25,140
Computational functionalism adds one more step. The relevant roles are computational.

40
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:32,680
Implement the right computation in neurons or in silicon and you get the mind, experience included.

41
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:38,960
That's the working assumption behind most research on AI consciousness and it's the view Aguera

42
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:43,860
E. Arcus defends. What about Daniel Dennett? One reply just said,

43
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:51,020
read Consciousness Explained. Daniel Dennett, who died in 2024, spent decades arguing that

44
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:57,580
the hard problem rests on a kind of cognitive illusion. His 1991 book was teasingly nicknamed

45
00:03:57,580 --> 00:04:02,460
Consciousness, Explained Away, but it's one of the most influential books in the field.

46
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:08,460
His position is now usually called illusionism, a name championed by the philosopher Keith

47
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:13,880
Illusionists agree that you're conscious, and that plenty is going on in your head.

48
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:19,780
Their claim is that phenomenal consciousness, in the philosopher's sense, does not really exist.

49
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,280
It only seems to.

50
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:26,620
That means private, indescribable inner qualities, what philosophers call qualia.

51
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:33,140
What does exist is a brain that represents its own states in a simplified and somewhat misleading way.

52
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:38,980
And that self-representation makes it seem as if there's an extra, inexplicable inner glow.

53
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:42,960
That sounds self-refuting. The seeming is the experience.

54
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:47,140
That's the standard objection, and illusionists reply carefully.

55
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:54,460
A seeming, they say, can be a representation, a functional state, without being a mysterious extra property.

56
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:58,800
But then who's being fooled? An illusion needs someone to have it.

57
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:03,780
The system itself, illusionists say, with no little observer watching inside.

58
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:08,000
Dennett borrowed a phrase from computing for this, the user illusion.

59
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:11,720
Your laptop's desktop shows folders and a trash can.

60
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:16,560
There are no tiny folders on the chip, but the interface is real, it's useful,

61
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:19,420
and it's the only way you ever deal with what's underneath.

62
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:23,760
On this view, the felt inner world is the brain's interface to itself.

63
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:29,180
Joe Carl Smith's essay Grokking Illusionism, which is linked in one of the replies,

64
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:33,560
is a wonderful account of what it's like to really try to take that seriously.

65
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:35,480
And here's the clever part.

66
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:39,960
Chalmers himself has proposed what he calls the metaproblem of consciousness.

67
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:42,280
Why do we think there's a hard problem?

68
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,680
Why do brains produce statements like,

69
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:47,000
I cannot explain my own experience?

70
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:51,600
That's an easy problem because it's about behavior, so everyone can work on it.

71
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:53,440
If we solve the meta problem,

72
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:56,060
and the explanation never needs to mention

73
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:57,760
genuinely phenomenal properties,

74
00:05:58,440 --> 00:05:59,960
illusionism looks much stronger.

75
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:03,200
If it does need them, illusionism looks weaker.

76
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:05,960
Wittgenstein, who shows up on your list too,

77
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:08,600
made a related diagnosis much earlier.

78
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:11,720
He noticed that the feeling of an unbridgeable gulf

79
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:13,800
between consciousness and brain processes

80
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,040
does not come up in ordinary life.

81
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:18,120
It appears when you turn your attention

82
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:20,680
on your own experience, in a philosopher's mood,

83
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:23,580
This is supposed to come from a brain.

84
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:27,620
He treated it as a confusion to dissolve, not a mystery to solve.

85
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:33,520
One of your repliers, Finn Morehouse, called illusionism the most compelling account of consciousness

86
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:38,020
and linked Frankish, Dennett, Carl Smith, and Chalmers on the metaproblem.

87
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:42,160
That cluster is the strongest deflationary position out there.

88
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:45,240
And it matters a lot for AI, as we'll see.

89
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:48,320
Where do Hofstadter and the strange loops fit?

90
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:54,700
Nearby, Douglas Hofstadter's Gerdel, Escher, Bach argues that a self is a strange loop,

91
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:57,220
a pattern that comes to represent itself.

92
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:00,220
Your thread loved it and also called it trash.

93
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:05,800
Honestly, it depends on whether you enjoy 700 pages of playful self-reference.

94
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:09,360
Thomas Metzinger's Being No One makes a related argument.

95
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:15,040
There is no self, he says, only a self-model that the brain cannot recognize as a model.

96
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:18,800
It's transparent, like a window you look through rather than at.

97
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:21,080
Being no one is very heavygoing.

98
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:25,060
His later book, The Ego Tunnel, is the accessible version.

99
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:30,180
And one reply recommends Peter Watts' novel Blindsight, which drew on Metzinger.

100
00:07:30,460 --> 00:07:34,960
It imagines aliens that are vastly intelligent and completely non-conscious,

101
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:40,340
and it asks whether consciousness might be a useless, even costly, side effect.

102
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:44,860
It's probably the best fictional treatment of the gap between doing and feeling.

103
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:49,300
Watts has made it free to read online under a Creative Commons license.

104
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:52,120
Okay, now the other side of the map.

105
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:55,140
First stop, biological naturalism.

106
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:59,420
John Searle argued that consciousness is a real biological phenomenon

107
00:07:59,420 --> 00:08:04,000
caused by specific powers of brains and that running a program is not enough.

108
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:06,560
That's the point of his famous Chinese room.

109
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:11,140
A person locked in a room, following a rulebook to shuffle Chinese symbols,

110
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:14,740
could produce perfect answers without understanding a word of Chinese.

111
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:20,440
As Searle liked to say, a computer simulation of a rainstorm does not get anything wet.

112
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:23,580
Anil Seth defends a modern version of this view.

113
00:08:24,380 --> 00:08:29,800
Consciousness, he suggests, may depend on being alive, on metabolism, and on the constant

114
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:33,200
work of self-maintenance that keeps an organism in one piece.

115
00:08:33,540 --> 00:08:40,180
His paper in the journal Behavioral and Brain Sciences on Conscious AI and Biological Naturalism

116
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:44,760
is the sharpest current statement of the case against computational functionalism.

117
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:48,660
Second stop, integrated information theory, or I.

118
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:49,740
I.T.

119
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:55,920
It comes from the neuroscientist Giulio Tononi, and Christoph Koch is its best-known champion.

120
00:08:56,280 --> 00:09:00,060
We'll cover the science next episode, but the philosophy is radical.

121
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:03,160
I.I.T. starts from the experience itself.

122
00:09:03,580 --> 00:09:08,860
Every experience exists for itself, is structured, is specific, is unified, and is definite.

123
00:09:09,220 --> 00:09:12,420
Then it asks what kind of physical system could have those properties.

124
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:16,880
Its answer is integrated information, measured by a quantity called phi.

125
00:09:17,540 --> 00:09:21,780
Roughly, phi measures how much a system's causal structure, taken as a whole,

126
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:26,640
constrains its own past and future, over and above what its parts do separately.

127
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:28,300
Here's the key move.

128
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:33,060
Phi is a property of physical causal structure, not of the software being run.

129
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:37,620
So a conventional digital computer that simulated your brain perfectly would,

130
00:09:37,820 --> 00:09:42,220
according to I, I, T, be almost entirely unconscious,

131
00:09:42,420 --> 00:09:45,200
because its hardware is not integrated in the right way.

132
00:09:45,340 --> 00:09:48,660
That's the subtitle of Koch's book, The Feeling of Life Itself.

133
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:52,420
Consciousness is widespread, but it cannot be computed.

134
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:55,840
And that's why one person in your thread recommended Koch

135
00:09:55,840 --> 00:09:58,420
as a challenge to computational functionalism,

136
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:03,640
while adding that they could not endorse I, IT, as it's constructed.

137
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:07,080
Then panpsychism, the Sean Carroll podcast.

138
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:08,280
Right.

139
00:10:08,280 --> 00:10:16,160
Panpsychism is the view that consciousness, in some extremely simple form, is a fundamental and widespread feature of matter.

140
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:19,440
Philip Goff is its best-known current advocate.

141
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:21,600
The motivation goes like this.

142
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:26,060
Physics tells us what matter does, mass, charge, how particles behave.

143
00:10:26,380 --> 00:10:28,940
It does not tell us what matter intrinsically is.

144
00:10:29,300 --> 00:10:33,720
Maybe its intrinsic nature is, in some minimal sense, experiential.

145
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:36,920
The big problem is what's called the combination problem.

146
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:44,760
of tiny experiences add up to one unified experience like yours right now. And what did

147
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:51,280
Carroll say? Carroll's objection is a physicist's. The laws that govern everyday matter have been

148
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:57,560
tested to extraordinary precision. If consciousness made any difference to what atoms do, those laws

149
00:10:57,560 --> 00:11:02,480
would have to be wrong somewhere we'd have noticed. And if it makes no difference to what atoms do,

150
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:06,680
it's hard to see how it could explain why we talk about consciousness at all.

151
00:11:08,280 --> 00:11:11,300
is that panpsychism does not change the equations at all.

152
00:11:11,640 --> 00:11:13,440
It's a claim about the inner nature

153
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:15,240
of whatever the equations describe.

154
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:18,220
It's an hour and a half of two smart people

155
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:19,700
disagreeing in good faith,

156
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:21,640
which is exactly what you want.

157
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:23,820
Then there are the positions further out.

158
00:11:24,460 --> 00:11:26,780
Idealism, defended by Bernardo Kastrup,

159
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:28,420
says mind is fundamental,

160
00:11:28,660 --> 00:11:31,360
and matter is what mind looks like from the outside.

161
00:11:32,020 --> 00:11:35,340
Dualism says experience is a fundamental feature of its own.

162
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:38,300
Chalmers has defended a naturalistic version,

163
00:11:38,300 --> 00:11:43,120
in which experience is tied to physical processes by laws we have not yet found.

164
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:47,020
Mysterianism, from Colin McGinn, says there's an answer,

165
00:11:47,180 --> 00:11:49,580
but our brains may not be built to grasp it.

166
00:11:49,980 --> 00:11:51,560
And the microtubules.

167
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:54,020
Wait, it's all microtubules?

168
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:55,360
Always has been.

169
00:11:55,620 --> 00:11:57,800
That meme is about ORCH-O, R,

170
00:11:58,120 --> 00:12:00,820
which stands for Orchestrated Objective Reduction.

171
00:12:01,420 --> 00:12:03,760
Roger Penrose and Stuart Hameroff

172
00:12:05,340 --> 00:12:11,040
arises from quantum processes in microtubules, the proteins scaffolding inside neurons.

173
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:16,920
Most neuroscientists are skeptical. The main objection is that the brain is warm and wet,

174
00:12:17,140 --> 00:12:21,060
so delicate quantum states there should fall apart far too quickly to matter.

175
00:12:21,540 --> 00:12:27,960
Supporters point to some intriguing results. In one 2024 study, a drug that stabilizes

176
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:33,780
microtubules delayed by about a minute, the point at which rats under anesthesia could no longer

177
00:12:35,340 --> 00:12:37,540
a standard stand-in for losing consciousness.

178
00:12:37,920 --> 00:12:40,240
That's suggestive, but far from decisive.

179
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:43,800
It was a small study, no quantum effects were measured,

180
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:48,620
and microtubules do plenty of ordinary, non-quantum work that could explain it.

181
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:51,880
Fun to read about, not where the mainstream is.

182
00:12:52,340 --> 00:12:55,060
Anything else from the thread before we sum up?

183
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:57,900
One more, because it's a great stress test.

184
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:00,180
Eric S. H. Witzgabel's paper,

185
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:04,380
If Materialism is True, the United States is Probably Conscious.

186
00:13:05,340 --> 00:13:10,960
the argument. Suppose you adopt criteria for consciousness that are liberal enough to include

187
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:16,240
rabbits and hypothetical intelligent aliens. Things like rich information integration,

188
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:22,660
sophisticated responsiveness to the environment, and self-monitoring. Then the United States,

189
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:28,840
taken as a single entity, seems to meet those criteria too. The reply that recommended it was

190
00:13:28,840 --> 00:13:34,860
not convinced by the conclusion, and most people are not. But it shows something important. Every

191
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:41,160
consciousness ends up implying something that sounds absurd. S.H. Witzgabel calls this the

192
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:46,920
weirdness of the world. Some crazy-sounding view has to be true, we just do not know which one.

193
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:53,180
Okay, give me the map in one picture. Picture a single question running through all of it.

194
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:59,000
Does the right pattern of information processing suffice for experience? Functionalists and

195
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:05,060
illusionists say yes in their different ways. And surprisingly, so does Chalmers. He thinks

196
00:14:05,340 --> 00:14:12,200
is tied by natural law to functional organization, so a silicon brain organized exactly like yours

197
00:14:12,200 --> 00:14:17,920
would be conscious too. The hard problem, in other words, does not by itself rule out conscious

198
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:25,420
machines. Biological naturalists say no, you need the right biology, maybe life itself. I, IT,

199
00:14:25,720 --> 00:14:31,380
says no, you need the right physical causal structure. Pansychists and idealists say the

200
00:14:31,380 --> 00:14:36,960
question is framed wrong because experience is there from the start. Where you land on that one

201
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:43,060
question determines almost everything you'll say about AI. And notice that none of these positions

202
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:49,340
has been settled by argument alone. So next episode, let's see what experiments can actually do.

